00:01:23.064 --> 00:01:28.269
And many of water's
properties are anomalous.

00:01:28.269 --> 00:01:32.039
And much of the reason for that
is the last type of bonding

00:01:32.039 --> 00:01:34.109
we talked about,
intermolecular bonds

00:01:34.109 --> 00:01:37.846
we talked about last Friday,
which is the hydrogen bond.

00:01:37.846 --> 00:01:41.649
So remember our table, I just
wanted to fill out the table

00:01:41.649 --> 00:01:43.651
because I didn't fill
out the table, right.

00:01:43.651 --> 00:01:45.019
That's the type of bond.

00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:48.890
And the model was some
kind of electronegative--

00:01:51.493 --> 00:01:54.429
it was some kind of highly
electronegative atom.

00:01:54.429 --> 00:01:59.768
Like a was oxygen or
nitrogen or fluorine.

00:01:59.768 --> 00:02:03.338
So that you drew a lot of
charge off of that hydrogen

00:02:03.338 --> 00:02:07.509
and you left it with a
really strong delta plus

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:12.013
so that it could then
bond to some other atom.

00:02:12.013 --> 00:02:13.581
It might be the
same, by the way.

00:02:13.581 --> 00:02:16.851
It was in the cases
that we talked about.

00:02:16.851 --> 00:02:20.355
Actually, I'll just use the same
atom because we use HF and H2O,

00:02:20.355 --> 00:02:22.257
so then it would
be the O or the F.

00:02:22.257 --> 00:02:26.361
But it's to a lone
pair on that atom.

00:02:26.361 --> 00:02:28.663
That was the hydrogen bond.

00:02:28.663 --> 00:02:37.572
And the range of attraction is
in kilojoules per mole of bonds

00:02:37.572 --> 00:02:42.143
is something like,
let's see, 10 to 40.

00:02:42.143 --> 00:02:44.045
So it's pretty strong,
you know, compared

00:02:44.045 --> 00:02:46.247
to some of the weaker
things like London,

00:02:46.247 --> 00:02:49.017
the hydrogen bond
can be pretty strong.

00:02:49.017 --> 00:02:52.120
And one of the examples
that is the classic example

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.289
would be

00:02:54.289 --> 00:02:59.727
Hydrogen, hydrogen, there's
the hydrogen bond right there,

00:02:59.727 --> 00:03:00.428
you see?

00:03:00.428 --> 00:03:05.834
Hydrogen bond between another
oxygen with its hydrogens

00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:06.367
as well.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:10.939
Now, we talked about
how water is so special

00:03:10.939 --> 00:03:15.677
because in the case of water,
not only do I have exactly two

00:03:15.677 --> 00:03:19.514
hydrogens in each molecule that
have caused this strong delta

00:03:19.514 --> 00:03:22.317
plus, but I've also
got two lone pairs.

00:03:22.317 --> 00:03:24.252
So the numbers work.

00:03:24.252 --> 00:03:27.989
In HF, that's also
a hydrogen bonded--

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:30.692
it can hydrogen bond
to itself, but I've

00:03:30.692 --> 00:03:33.228
got three lone pairs on
each F and only one hydrogen

00:03:33.228 --> 00:03:34.796
that I've taken that
charge away from.

00:03:34.796 --> 00:03:36.397
So you still get the
hydrogen bonding,

00:03:36.397 --> 00:03:39.367
but this is the
beautiful balance.

00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:43.905
And there's a video showing
water bonded to each other.

00:03:43.905 --> 00:03:45.473
Oh, there it is.

00:03:45.473 --> 00:03:48.409
Because there's nothing like
just imagining what a hydrogen

00:03:48.409 --> 00:03:49.477
bond looks like.

00:03:49.477 --> 00:03:52.513
And so of course, a
hydrogen bond will--

00:03:52.513 --> 00:03:53.448
OK, charges.

00:03:53.448 --> 00:03:54.482
Oh, it's French.

00:03:54.482 --> 00:03:58.186
[SPEAKING FRENCH] OK.

00:03:58.186 --> 00:03:59.754
Oh, that's what it looks like.

00:03:59.754 --> 00:04:01.256
Really, if you could
get down there,

00:04:01.256 --> 00:04:05.159
it would be a ray of
a laser beam light.

00:04:05.159 --> 00:04:08.396
It's a liaison hydrogene.

00:04:08.396 --> 00:04:11.132
And there it is.

00:04:11.132 --> 00:04:13.368
And this is what's
going on in the liquid.

00:04:13.368 --> 00:04:14.802
They're kind of
seeing each other.

00:04:14.802 --> 00:04:15.837
And they're talking.

00:04:15.837 --> 00:04:20.575
But they feel this strong,
strong hydrogen bond.

00:04:20.575 --> 00:04:22.243
And that's happening
all over the place.

00:04:22.243 --> 00:04:26.881
And in a liquid, they are making
and breaking all the time.

00:04:26.881 --> 00:04:28.249
Question?

00:04:28.249 --> 00:04:30.752
STUDENT: Why do they separate?

00:04:30.752 --> 00:04:31.419
Yeah.

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:33.554
Yeah, because--
well, it depends.

00:04:33.554 --> 00:04:34.155
It depends.

00:04:34.155 --> 00:04:35.623
It's a great question.

00:04:35.623 --> 00:04:38.092
Why do they separate?

00:04:38.092 --> 00:04:40.662
Well, they don't in ice.

00:04:40.662 --> 00:04:43.298
So in ice, I mean,
you know, they can.

00:04:43.298 --> 00:04:47.302
But in ice, it's a solid, so
they're sort of locked in.

00:04:47.302 --> 00:04:50.805
And so in there, you're going
to have pretty much each water

00:04:50.805 --> 00:04:52.206
molecule has four--

00:04:52.206 --> 00:04:53.374
look at this.

00:04:53.374 --> 00:04:55.510
It's you know from VESPR, right?

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:58.313
Four possible bonds.

00:04:58.313 --> 00:05:00.548
You can make tetrahedral.

00:05:00.548 --> 00:05:01.916
But there's actually
lots of ways

00:05:01.916 --> 00:05:03.584
you can orient them in a solid.

00:05:03.584 --> 00:05:06.554
There's many forms of
ice, 12 different ways

00:05:06.554 --> 00:05:08.723
to make a solid of ice.

00:05:08.723 --> 00:05:12.994
But see, here-- gesundheit.

00:05:12.994 --> 00:05:16.097
So here in the liquid
at room temperature,

00:05:16.097 --> 00:05:20.268
with the laser beams, they're
coming in and they're bonding,

00:05:20.268 --> 00:05:23.438
but they have enough energy
to break the bond, too.

00:05:23.438 --> 00:05:25.773
So they stick and they
unstick because there's

00:05:25.773 --> 00:05:29.510
enough thermal energy in the
system to continuously break

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:30.445
them and remake them.

00:05:30.445 --> 00:05:33.281
That's when it's a liquid.

00:05:33.281 --> 00:05:35.850
When is it a liquid
versus a gas?

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:37.752
Right, the boiling
point, remember,

00:05:37.752 --> 00:05:40.421
that is a proxy we
talked about last Friday.

00:05:40.421 --> 00:05:43.124
That is a proxy for
the bonding strength

00:05:43.124 --> 00:05:45.426
between these molecules.

00:05:45.426 --> 00:05:48.363
If it's a stronger bonding,
then the boiling point

00:05:48.363 --> 00:05:49.964
will be higher and higher.

00:05:49.964 --> 00:05:53.735
Oh, water should have boiled
at a much lower temperature

00:05:53.735 --> 00:05:56.604
if it didn't have this very
special hydrogen bond, which

00:05:56.604 --> 00:05:58.740
is not a dipole-dipole.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:02.243
People try to write the physics
of the hydrogen bond as 1

00:06:02.243 --> 00:06:03.011
over r cubed.

00:06:03.011 --> 00:06:04.245
That is not true.

00:06:04.245 --> 00:06:08.383
That is not just as simple as,
it's another dipole-dipole type

00:06:08.383 --> 00:06:09.050
interaction.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.452
It's complicated.

00:06:11.452 --> 00:06:14.155
Complicated.

00:06:14.155 --> 00:06:18.326
Remember, we had covalent
bonds were complicated too.

00:06:18.326 --> 00:06:21.062
So we're not going
to put a simple one

00:06:21.062 --> 00:06:24.932
over r something dependence
for the hydrogen bond.

00:06:24.932 --> 00:06:26.401
OK, good question.

00:06:26.401 --> 00:06:29.637
OK now, all right, so that's
an intermolecular-- now

00:06:29.637 --> 00:06:31.372
