00:06:46.387 --> 00:06:48.890
Here's a question
from a quiz last year.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:52.460
List the intermolecular forces
in each substance, methane,

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:54.829
and then you've got that CH3OH.

00:06:54.829 --> 00:06:57.965
And then you've got the CH3CL.

00:06:57.965 --> 00:07:01.035
And so you can do this
now because for methane

00:07:01.035 --> 00:07:03.071
all you've got is London.

00:07:03.071 --> 00:07:04.272
It's not a polar molecule.

00:07:04.272 --> 00:07:05.373
All you've got is London.

00:07:05.373 --> 00:07:08.443
There's no hydrogen bond.

00:07:08.443 --> 00:07:14.682
But for CH3OH, do you
have hydrogen bonding?

00:07:14.682 --> 00:07:15.817
Yeah, you do.

00:07:15.817 --> 00:07:17.018
Because if you draw--

00:07:17.018 --> 00:07:20.888
because you've got an H
that's had the charge taken

00:07:20.888 --> 00:07:25.827
from it by oxygen, a highly
electronegative atom, and then

00:07:25.827 --> 00:07:28.262
and then you've got lone
pairs on that same oxygen.

00:07:28.262 --> 00:07:32.600
So you can hydrogen bond
and you've also got London.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:33.868
All right, now is that it?

00:07:33.868 --> 00:07:35.970
Is there anything else?

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:37.138
CH3OH.

00:07:37.138 --> 00:07:37.638
What else?

00:07:37.638 --> 00:07:41.542
Is there any other type of bond?

00:07:41.542 --> 00:07:43.311
Well, the last question
you need to ask

00:07:43.311 --> 00:07:46.447
is, is there a dipole
moment in the molecule?

00:07:46.447 --> 00:07:49.217
Because if that molecule
had a permanent dipole,

00:07:49.217 --> 00:07:51.719
then there is another type of
intermolecular interaction,

00:07:51.719 --> 00:07:53.821
like we talked
about, dipole-dipole.

00:07:53.821 --> 00:07:57.091
So you'd have three.

00:07:57.091 --> 00:08:03.231
And for CH3CO, well there, you
also-- you know that for CH3CO,

00:08:03.231 --> 00:08:04.765
you're going to have
a dipole moment.

00:08:04.765 --> 00:08:08.002
Remember, we compared
dipole moments of molecules.

00:08:08.002 --> 00:08:09.904
And if you have a diaper
moment in a molecule,

00:08:09.904 --> 00:08:13.074
then that dipole moment can
talk to another dipole moment

00:08:13.074 --> 00:08:14.709
and be interacting with it.

00:08:14.709 --> 00:08:16.711
So that's a kind of bond.

00:08:16.711 --> 00:08:17.879
And is that it?

00:08:21.149 --> 00:08:24.385
London, London is always there.

00:08:24.385 --> 00:08:25.453
It's always there.

00:08:25.453 --> 00:08:27.421
We've also got
London, don't forget.

00:08:27.421 --> 00:08:30.892
So you'd have dipole-dipole
plus London here.

00:08:30.892 --> 00:08:35.696
Dipole-dipole London and
hydrogen, just London.

00:08:35.696 --> 00:08:37.698
So this is the kind
of way I want--

00:08:37.698 --> 00:08:39.500
and you might be able
to think about, well,

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:41.434
would a trend-- if
you think about,

00:08:41.434 --> 00:08:43.236
like, OK well, if
a molecule just

00:08:43.236 --> 00:08:47.742
has London, then what
does London depend on?

00:08:47.742 --> 00:08:51.479
All right, well, it depends
on the polarizability

00:08:51.479 --> 00:08:52.180
of the charge.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:53.748
So that can be
something [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:53.748 --> 00:08:56.417
It depends on the size because
that tells you how much--

00:08:56.417 --> 00:09:01.889
if I say, well, what about
SIH4 compared to CH4?

00:09:01.889 --> 00:09:05.092
Which is going to be
more strongly bonded?

00:09:05.092 --> 00:09:06.861
You can answer that
because you know

00:09:06.861 --> 00:09:08.629
the only force there is London.

00:09:08.629 --> 00:09:12.333
And you know for London, it's
how much contact area you have

00:09:12.333 --> 00:09:13.768
is going to be one
of the factors.

00:09:13.768 --> 00:09:16.037
The more contact area, the
more chances these things

00:09:16.037 --> 00:09:18.573
can get close
enough and polarize.

00:09:18.573 --> 00:09:22.443
And then polarizability
would be the other.

