you see there where

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.075
you've got your 1s and your 1s.

00:13:15.075 --> 00:13:16.644
I'm not even going
to fill these out,

00:13:16.644 --> 00:13:19.747
and they're sigma
and sigma star.

00:13:19.747 --> 00:13:24.452
And so that would be like two
1s orbitals that I have either--

00:13:24.452 --> 00:13:27.354
OK, so up here, I took one.

00:13:27.354 --> 00:13:30.257
It was a positive
and I subtracted one.

00:13:30.257 --> 00:13:35.229
And here, I took
one and I added one.

00:13:35.229 --> 00:13:38.899
And remember, this
gave us this and this.

00:13:38.899 --> 00:13:42.803
And this gave us the
bonding orbital, right?

00:13:42.803 --> 00:13:44.805
Bonding, anti-bonding.

00:13:44.805 --> 00:13:46.807
It all came from LCAO.

00:13:46.807 --> 00:13:49.677
LCAO is the linear combination
of atomic orbitals.

00:13:49.677 --> 00:13:51.111
That's the theory
we use to build

00:13:51.111 --> 00:13:52.947
up our [INAUDIBLE] theory.

00:13:52.947 --> 00:13:56.283
But now, we've got
to go a lot further.

00:13:56.283 --> 00:13:58.486
So I'm going to--
because I don't

00:13:58.486 --> 00:14:02.556
want to keep adding things here
and here, it gets complicated.

00:14:02.556 --> 00:14:08.295
I'm trying to find the MOs for
more, and more, and more atoms

00:14:08.295 --> 00:14:12.132
because I've got to put a
whole lot into my solid.

00:14:12.132 --> 00:14:14.301
So I'm going to write
them just on one side.

00:14:14.301 --> 00:14:19.139
If I write the two
here like that,

00:14:19.139 --> 00:14:21.308
and that would be
sigma and sigma star,

00:14:21.308 --> 00:14:22.843
then you know what
I'm talking about.

00:14:22.843 --> 00:14:24.378
I'm taking the two
and I'm combining them

00:14:24.378 --> 00:14:25.412
in two different ways.

00:14:25.412 --> 00:14:27.014
But what if I had three?

00:14:27.014 --> 00:14:29.550
Well see, if I had three--

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:32.486
so I've got three
s orbitals now.

00:14:32.486 --> 00:14:38.392
So if I've got three s orbitals,
then if you think about it,

00:14:38.392 --> 00:14:43.330
I could also add them like that.

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:45.966
That would be a very
nice bonding orbital.

00:14:45.966 --> 00:14:47.668
If you add them,
you see that they're

00:14:47.668 --> 00:14:50.204
going to have the same kind
of really nice bonding.

00:14:50.204 --> 00:14:52.940
But I can also subtract
one in the middle.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:55.075
And if I did that--

00:14:55.075 --> 00:14:59.480
so I had a plus,
minus, plus there.

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:03.784
So those are the signs,
plus s, minus s, plus s.

00:15:03.784 --> 00:15:07.288
Then you can see that in-between
those, as I add these together,

00:15:07.288 --> 00:15:09.023
you have nodes.

00:15:09.023 --> 00:15:12.760
So this is not going to look
like a very happy bonding

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:13.427
situation.

00:15:13.427 --> 00:15:15.195
I'm going to have
something in there

00:15:15.195 --> 00:15:16.964
and then something in there.

00:15:16.964 --> 00:15:19.466
That's what that's going to
look like as I bring them closer

00:15:19.466 --> 00:15:20.701
and try to add them.

00:15:20.701 --> 00:15:23.671
I made two nodes here.

00:15:23.671 --> 00:15:26.140
Well, there's one other
way you could do this.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:29.276
And we know that the number
of molecular orbitals

00:15:29.276 --> 00:15:32.279
has to equal the number
of atomic orbitals.

00:15:32.279 --> 00:15:33.013
We know that.

00:15:33.013 --> 00:15:34.982
So there's one
more that we mean,

00:15:34.982 --> 00:15:37.217
because I started
with three s's.

00:15:37.217 --> 00:15:39.019
And you can kind of see that.

00:15:39.019 --> 00:15:44.325
You can see that because
if I had a plus and a plus,

00:15:44.325 --> 00:15:47.394
if I had done it that way
or maybe the other way,

00:15:47.394 --> 00:15:49.597
maybe this was
over there, but you

00:15:49.597 --> 00:15:52.499
see I got a little bonding
going on in here between them.

00:15:52.499 --> 00:15:54.835
And I got an anti-bonding there.

00:15:54.835 --> 00:15:56.837
It's almost like it
kind of cancels out.

00:15:56.837 --> 00:16:00.674
So you can imagine that
that combination of s

00:16:00.674 --> 00:16:02.710
orbitals, if I add and
subtract them this way,

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:05.913
it's going to be somewhere
right in the middle.

00:16:05.913 --> 00:16:08.549
Maybe not so bonding,
maybe not so anti-bonding.

00:16:08.549 --> 00:16:11.018
OK, good.

00:16:11.018 --> 00:16:13.053
All right, well let's do four.

00:16:13.053 --> 00:16:16.624
So if I had four, if
I started with four--

00:16:16.624 --> 00:16:18.859
this is like four
hydrogen atoms, let's say,

00:16:18.859 --> 00:16:20.361
although I'm not filling it yet.

00:16:20.361 --> 00:16:23.263
But so four 1s orbitals.

00:16:23.263 --> 00:16:26.767
Well now you see,
I could do this.

00:16:26.767 --> 00:16:30.004
I can add them all up and get
this super awesome bonding

00:16:30.004 --> 00:16:30.504
state.

00:16:33.674 --> 00:16:39.813
I could also alternate
them so that everything

00:16:39.813 --> 00:16:41.649
is anti-bonding.

00:16:41.649 --> 00:16:42.716
Look at that.

00:16:42.716 --> 00:16:45.252
These are going to cancel
as you bring them together

00:16:45.252 --> 00:16:48.288
and you're going to get
anti-bonding, anti-bonding.

00:16:48.288 --> 00:16:53.027
But there's also two other
options that are in between.

00:16:53.027 --> 00:16:58.132
Right here, I can add
2 and then subtract 2.

00:16:58.132 --> 00:17:00.868
And you can see, these
are both negative.

00:17:00.868 --> 00:17:02.269
And as they come
together, they're

00:17:02.269 --> 00:17:04.704
going to overlap
constructively, so will these.

00:17:04.704 --> 00:17:06.406
But in the middle
here, they're going

00:17:06.406 --> 00:17:10.277
to be kind of
destructive interference.

00:17:10.277 --> 00:17:13.614
And so you're going to have
this one node in between.

00:17:13.614 --> 00:17:15.182
But in general, you
can imagine, this

00:17:15.182 --> 00:17:17.617
will be more bonding
than anti-bonding,

00:17:17.617 --> 00:17:19.853
so we draw it down
below the middle.

00:17:19.853 --> 00:17:23.156
Whereas up here, you could
also imagine something

00:17:23.156 --> 00:17:26.026
like this, where you
start with a plus

00:17:26.026 --> 00:17:29.830
and you put two minuses in
there and a plus over there.

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:31.498
I'm just adding and subtracting.

00:17:31.498 --> 00:17:34.334
I'm just taking LCAO
theory and moving it

00:17:34.334 --> 00:17:37.171
to more than two orbitals.

00:17:37.171 --> 00:17:40.340
OK, so now you can
actually understand this

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:41.742
from a number of nodes, right?

00:17:41.742 --> 00:17:45.079
This is like three nodes.

00:17:45.079 --> 00:17:49.883
This is two nodes over there.

00:17:49.883 --> 00:17:53.287
This is one node.

00:17:53.287 --> 00:17:55.456
And zero nodes.

00:17:55.456 --> 00:17:57.458
This is exactly
the kind of thing

00:17:57.458 --> 00:17:59.893
we talked about already,
but for only two orbitals.

00:17:59.893 --> 00:18:02.396
Now we're just
continuing it along.

00:18:02.396 --> 00:18:07.000
OK, you can see why the good
old days are over, because see,

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:10.370
now we've got to
jump this up a level.

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:12.840
And we've got to think,
how many s orbitals

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:16.243
do I have in a typical
piece of matter?

00:18:16.243 --> 00:18:19.680
How many do you think?

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:20.347
Take a guess.

00:18:23.851 --> 00:18:25.552
Roughly?

00:18:25.552 --> 00:18:28.222
Take a chunk of
hydrogen this big.

00:18:28.222 --> 00:18:31.825
How many atoms do I have?

00:18:31.825 --> 00:18:33.494
A mole, exactly.

00:18:33.494 --> 00:18:36.430
That's why we
invented the mole, so

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:41.568
that we could count big numbers
by just saying 1, 1 mole.

00:18:41.568 --> 00:18:43.403
But if I have a mole,
then I've got like 10

00:18:43.403 --> 00:18:45.272
to the 24th of these.

00:18:45.272 --> 00:18:47.474
I just went to four.

00:18:47.474 --> 00:18:49.777
I just went to four.

00:18:49.777 --> 00:18:51.378
But it just keeps on going.

00:18:51.378 --> 00:18:52.713
So [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:52.713 --> 00:18:57.785
So here is like, OK,
1, orbital of atom.

00:18:57.785 --> 00:18:59.553
Boy, I labeled that well.

00:18:59.553 --> 00:19:02.756
2, bonding, anti-bonding
orbitals of molecules.

00:19:02.756 --> 00:19:04.992
And then you see, OK, there's 3.

00:19:04.992 --> 00:19:06.760
There's 4, 5.

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:08.362
Then you keep on going.

00:19:08.362 --> 00:19:09.863
OK, they got a
little tired and then

00:19:09.863 --> 00:19:15.135
they wrote 30, 30 orbitals.

00:19:15.135 --> 00:19:17.971
30 is still way, way far away.

00:19:17.971 --> 00:19:21.175
But if you had 30s
orbitals, you might still

00:19:21.175 --> 00:19:24.178
be able to kind of see
these lines discretely.

00:19:24.178 --> 00:19:25.512
Remember, this is energy space.

00:19:25.512 --> 00:19:27.080
There might actually--
remember, like,

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:28.749
these energies are
really important,

00:19:28.749 --> 00:19:30.250
the lines between the deltas.

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:35.055
But if you have a mole,
if you have a mole,

00:19:35.055 --> 00:19:37.424
it looks like one
continuous set of lines.

00:19:37.424 --> 00:19:41.562
These don't really
look discrete anymore.

00:19:41.562 --> 00:19:44.131
These don't look discrete.

00:19:44.131 --> 00:19:49.369
And so if you have a mole of
atoms, so if you have a solid,

00:19:49.369 --> 00:19:54.441
we call that collection
of orbitals that

00:19:54.441 --> 00:19:56.977
are all stuck in here a band.

00:19:56.977 --> 00:19:59.213
We call that a band.

00:19:59.213 --> 00:20:01.348
OK, so if I take--

00:20:01.348 --> 00:20:07.054
Now, I'm not going to
draw 10 to the 24th.

00:20:07.054 --> 00:20:09.790
10 to the 24th s orbitals.

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:14.628
But pretend, pretend that
that's what I have here.

00:20:14.628 --> 00:20:16.029
Then what happens?

00:20:18.866 --> 00:20:23.136
What I do is I form a continuum
of states, a continuum

00:20:23.136 --> 00:20:25.172
of levels for those electrons.

00:20:25.172 --> 00:20:28.041
And this, they're
so packed together,

00:20:28.041 --> 00:20:29.409
we don't even draw
them as lines.

00:20:29.409 --> 00:20:30.978
We can't.

00:20:30.978 --> 00:20:35.249
So this is called the 1s band.

00:20:35.249 --> 00:20:37.417
Oh, band.

00:20:37.417 --> 00:20:38.752
It's a band.

00:20:38.752 --> 00:20:40.754
It's a band of states.

00:20:40.754 --> 00:20:42.723
It's a band of states.

00:20:42.723 --> 00:20:45.492
It's the 1s band.

00:20:45.492 --> 00:20:49.029
Now, there are some
important rules

00:20:49.029 --> 00:20:53.100
that are really just the same
but we follow them here too.

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:55.035
And so I want to emphasize them.

00:20:55.035 --> 00:20:57.070
We've already learned
them for molecules.

00:20:57.070 --> 00:20:59.840
They holds for bands and solids.

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:05.545
All right, so now, for example,
let's think about this.

00:21:05.545 --> 00:21:11.919
Each of these orbitals could
occupy how many electrons?

00:21:11.919 --> 00:21:13.220
Two.

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:21.929
So I have two electrons
for each s orbital, s orb.

00:21:21.929 --> 00:21:28.168
Well, you know, if you think
about that, then for n atoms,

00:21:28.168 --> 00:21:30.871
and here what did I
say, 10 to the 24.

00:21:30.871 --> 00:21:34.241
Because there is one s orbital
that comes from each atom.

00:21:34.241 --> 00:21:42.883
So for n atoms, then I
would have 2n electrons

00:21:42.883 --> 00:21:49.289
can go into the one s band.

00:21:49.289 --> 00:21:51.091
So if I have--

00:21:51.091 --> 00:21:52.192
that makes sense, right?

00:21:52.192 --> 00:21:54.628
Because I had two for each
orbital, and I had that many.

00:21:54.628 --> 00:22:00.233
So if I know how many atoms I
have that make up this solid,

00:22:00.233 --> 00:22:03.136
then that's how many electrons
that band can have in it.

00:22:03.136 --> 00:22:07.007
All we did is go from the atomic
orbital picture to the solid

00:22:07.007 --> 00:22:08.675
to the mole.

00:22:08.675 --> 00:22:10.978
OK, why am I saying that?

00:22:10.978 --> 00:22:17.818
Because filling is
incredibly important

00:22:17.818 --> 00:22:19.920
And it happens in the
same way-- just like it

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:22.389
was so important for
molecular orbitals,

00:22:22.389 --> 00:22:24.424
it's also critical for bands.

00:22:24.424 --> 00:22:29.896
So filling is like MOs.

00:22:29.896 --> 00:22:39.639
It's just like in MOs,
start from lowest energy.

00:22:43.777 --> 00:22:44.911
Then fill up.

00:22:48.081 --> 00:22:50.584
So you know, if you
think about this,

00:22:50.584 --> 00:22:53.020
if I had-- now I'm
going to actually say,

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:56.390
well, OK, I made the one s band
by combining these orbitals.

00:22:56.390 --> 00:22:58.592
Now I'm going to
see what I have,

00:22:58.592 --> 00:23:00.060
which tells me how to fill it.

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:08.668
Now I have hydrogen. So
if this is the one s band

00:23:08.668 --> 00:23:17.944
and I have hydrogen, hydrogen
has, for each hydrogen,

00:23:17.944 --> 00:23:20.180
I've got one electron.

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.682
But I just said that
in the one band,

00:23:22.682 --> 00:23:24.885
for each atom I could
have put two electrons.

00:23:24.885 --> 00:23:28.522
So you know if this is made
of hydrogen, it's half filled,

00:23:28.522 --> 00:23:32.292
which is exactly what
that picture shows.

00:23:32.292 --> 00:23:33.093
That's the filling.

00:23:33.093 --> 00:23:34.728
You see it's filled half way.

00:23:34.728 --> 00:23:36.530
The band is filled halfway.

00:23:36.530 --> 00:23:43.103
It's like the MO
picture, but it's not

00:23:43.103 --> 00:23:45.372
because I've made a
continuous band out of it.

00:23:45.372 --> 00:23:47.874
So instead of putting
lines here, lines there,

00:23:47.874 --> 00:23:50.143
and then occupying
them with arrows,

00:23:50.143 --> 00:23:55.682
I fill them up with 10
to the 24-ish electrons.

00:23:55.682 --> 00:23:56.983
So we fill bands up like that.

00:23:56.983 --> 00:23:58.351
And we know that
it's half filled

00:23:58.351 --> 00:24:01.188
because it's hydrogen.
Another thing

00:24:01.188 --> 00:24:05.492
that we know is
that the energy--

00:24:05.492 --> 00:24:08.228
remember this?

00:24:08.228 --> 00:24:12.599
This is also important.

00:24:12.599 --> 00:24:20.707
That energy is related to
interaction and overlap.

00:24:20.707 --> 00:24:26.446
This is something we talked
about, interaction and overlap

00:24:26.446 --> 00:24:27.547
of the orbitals.

00:24:27.547 --> 00:24:33.253
How much does that band
go up and down in energy?

00:24:33.253 --> 00:24:34.788
This is something
we've talked about.

00:24:34.788 --> 00:24:36.490
How much that band
goes up and in energy,

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:41.228
it's just like in
the H2 molecule.

00:24:41.228 --> 00:24:43.396
One s, one s,
sigma, sigma, star.

00:24:43.396 --> 00:24:45.899
How far apart are
sigma star and energy?

00:24:45.899 --> 00:24:48.268
Well, it depends on the
interaction strength

00:24:48.268 --> 00:24:51.004
and the overlap
between those orbitals.

00:24:51.004 --> 00:24:53.507
The same is true in solids.

00:24:53.507 --> 00:24:54.541
Same is true in solids.

00:24:54.541 --> 00:24:59.212
So what is the
width of that band?

00:24:59.212 --> 00:25:02.182
How spread out
over energy is it?

00:25:02.182 --> 00:25:05.619
OK, so those are
some ground rules.

00:25:05.619 --> 00:25:08.054
Now, what else can we say?

00:25:08.054 --> 00:25:10.457
OK, so there's the
absolute least bonding

00:25:10.457 --> 00:25:13.560
state, anti-bonding, all the
way up there, the most bonding.

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:16.029
And then you've got
almost infinite number

00:25:16.029 --> 00:25:17.297
of possibilities in between.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:27.073
But the thing is that filling
determines the properties.

00:25:27.073 --> 00:25:31.745
You can't just know the
bands or the band structure,

00:25:31.745 --> 00:25:33.813
as it's called.

00:25:33.813 --> 00:25:35.549
But you have to
know the filling.

00:25:35.549 --> 00:25:39.386
And when you have a
situation like this--

00:25:39.386 --> 00:25:40.820
so let's write this down.

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:50.096
When you have partial
filling, partially

00:25:50.096 --> 00:25:55.535
filled band is a metal.

00:25:59.339 --> 00:26:02.742
And I will talk about
metals on Friday.

00:26:02.742 --> 00:26:04.978
So we will come
back to that concept

00:26:04.978 --> 00:26:09.249
and the electronic structure
of metals on Friday.

00:26:09.249 --> 00:26:13.053
But today, I want to talk about
something else that happens,

00:26:13.053 --> 00:26:18.325
which is when you fill not
in the middle of a band,

00:26:18.325 --> 00:26:20.860
not in the middle, but maybe
all the way up to the top.

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:22.562
And there's a gap.

00:26:22.562 --> 00:26:25.098
That's a different
kind of material.

00:26:25.098 --> 00:26:27.400
So if filling stops somewhere
partially in the band,

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:29.202
in the middle of a
band, if you're filling,

00:26:29.202 --> 00:26:32.772
you're counting, and you
didn't go all the way up,

00:26:32.772 --> 00:26:35.508
then you have a metal.

00:26:35.508 --> 00:26:36.876
But the thing is,
here we've only

00:26:36.876 --> 00:26:39.045
talked about one kind of band.

00:26:39.045 --> 00:26:40.547
This is the s band.

00:26:40.547 --> 00:26:44.718
But every single one of these--

00:26:44.718 --> 00:26:48.221
look at this, I can
take a mole of orbitals

00:26:48.221 --> 00:26:51.458
and make an s band, like that.

00:26:51.458 --> 00:26:53.159
And then, oh, that was 1s.

00:26:53.159 --> 00:26:55.262
Here's 2s.

00:26:55.262 --> 00:27:01.935
And look at this, I can take the
p electrons and make a p band.

00:27:01.935 --> 00:27:03.236
2p band.

00:27:03.236 --> 00:27:05.972
I can do this with
every single--

00:27:05.972 --> 00:27:09.175
2s, 1s, right?

00:27:09.175 --> 00:27:16.349
So depending on a
bunch of factors,

00:27:16.349 --> 00:27:20.053
these bands could
look different.

00:27:20.053 --> 00:27:21.221
What does it depend on?

00:27:21.221 --> 00:27:26.693
So this is-- the way these
bands are organized, right,

00:27:26.693 --> 00:27:30.230
we know now that
these orbitals will

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:35.068
come together like I've drawn
there and there and form bands.

00:27:35.068 --> 00:27:38.038
What is the difference
between these?

00:27:38.038 --> 00:27:40.874
Did they overlap, maybe?

00:27:40.874 --> 00:27:43.243
Are they very far in energy?

00:27:43.243 --> 00:27:46.880
These things depend on atom--

00:27:50.050 --> 00:28:01.728
depends on the atom and the
bonding and the structure.

00:28:08.368 --> 00:28:10.870
How do those bands look?

00:28:10.870 --> 00:28:12.339
Well, it depends.

00:28:12.339 --> 00:28:14.808
And we'll be talking
about some examples.

00:28:14.808 --> 00:28:20.847
But for now, I want to focus
on that filling thing again.

00:28:20.847 --> 00:28:25.051
What happens if I--

00:28:25.051 --> 00:28:28.488
if I fill this 1s up to
half way, it's a metal.

00:28:28.488 --> 00:28:30.657
If I keep filling,
keep adding electrons,

00:28:30.657 --> 00:28:34.728
and I fill this up
halfway, it's a metal.

00:28:34.728 --> 00:28:39.432
But if I stop filling,
you know, if I fill this

00:28:39.432 --> 00:28:43.970
and then I fill this, and then
there's some space in energy

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:46.940
and I didn't fill any
more, that's not a metal.

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:48.408
That's not a metal.

00:28:48.408 --> 00:28:49.542
So if there's a gap--

00:28:49.542 --> 00:28:52.879
so there can be gaps between
bands, which I just showed you.

00:28:52.879 --> 00:28:56.149
That's between an
s and a p band.

00:28:56.149 --> 00:28:58.118
And then the filling
determines the properties.

00:28:58.118 --> 00:28:59.886
And they're so important.

00:28:59.886 --> 00:29:01.354
They're so important.

00:29:01.354 --> 00:29:02.422
So let's talk about that.

00:29:02.422 --> 00:29:03.857
I want to talk about these.

00:29:03.857 --> 00:29:05.959
What we're going to
do is we're talking

00:29:05.959 --> 00:29:12.031
about semiconductors today
and Wednesday, and metals

00:29:12.031 --> 00:29:13.600
on Friday.

00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:15.969
And we're going to see it
through the vantage point

00:29:15.969 --> 00:29:17.604
of these electronic levels.

00:29:17.604 --> 00:29:19.773
And then next week, we'll
see it through the vantage

00:29:19.773 --> 00:29:21.074
point of atoms.

00:29:24.444 --> 00:29:29.182
So, if you have a gap between--

00:29:29.182 --> 00:29:30.850
well, you always have
gaps, the question,

00:29:30.850 --> 00:29:33.086
again, comes to filling.

00:29:33.086 --> 00:29:34.154
If the filling stops--

00:29:39.192 --> 00:29:42.228
I want to define this
very important-- so

00:29:42.228 --> 00:29:47.066
if the filling stops,
then you have this highest

00:29:47.066 --> 00:29:48.101
occupied level.

00:29:48.101 --> 00:29:51.371
So let's suppose
I have filling--

00:29:51.371 --> 00:29:53.740
this is some band.

00:29:53.740 --> 00:29:55.408
And this is some band.

00:29:55.408 --> 00:29:58.311
And this is unoccupied.

00:29:58.311 --> 00:30:02.348
So unoccupied by electrons.

00:30:02.348 --> 00:30:06.486
This is fully
occupied by electrons.

00:30:09.489 --> 00:30:11.157
So what's going to happen?

00:30:11.157 --> 00:30:14.494
Well, first of all, let's
just get some terms.

00:30:14.494 --> 00:30:18.531
The last band that I filled,
the last band that I filled,

00:30:18.531 --> 00:30:19.899
is called the valence band.

00:30:24.037 --> 00:30:26.306
Valence band.

00:30:26.306 --> 00:30:29.609
And we use vb for short.

00:30:29.609 --> 00:30:33.246
And the first band
that I don't fill

00:30:33.246 --> 00:30:35.215
is called the conduction band.

00:30:35.215 --> 00:30:40.353
And as we talk more
about these materials,

00:30:40.353 --> 00:30:42.755
you will see why
they're called that.

00:30:42.755 --> 00:30:43.423
Conduction band.

00:30:46.759 --> 00:30:50.029
But for now, just trust
me in the labeling.

00:30:50.029 --> 00:30:53.233
So the conduction band
and the valence band

00:30:53.233 --> 00:30:55.235
depend on the filling.

00:30:55.235 --> 00:30:58.805
Because again, it's, you
know, it's where I filled to.

00:30:58.805 --> 00:31:02.842
Now, the top level, the very,
very top level out of these 10

00:31:02.842 --> 00:31:07.046
to the 24 level, that very
top one has a special name.

00:31:07.046 --> 00:31:10.383
It's the vb maximum.

00:31:10.383 --> 00:31:11.384
That makes sense, right?

00:31:11.384 --> 00:31:15.321
It's the top valence
band, or the vbn.

00:31:15.321 --> 00:31:18.224
And the one down here
also has a special name,

00:31:18.224 --> 00:31:27.000
it's the cb minimum, or cbm.

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:30.236
So the valence band maximum
and the conduction band minimum

00:31:30.236 --> 00:31:32.572
are, you know, it tells
you, if you think about it,

00:31:32.572 --> 00:31:35.508
it tells you if you
subtracted those energies

00:31:35.508 --> 00:31:38.111
between those parts
of the band, you'd

00:31:38.111 --> 00:31:40.380
get the distance and energy
between the two bands.

00:31:43.550 --> 00:31:46.085
So the conduction band
minimum is this energy level

00:31:46.085 --> 00:31:48.721
here and the valence
band maximum energy is--

00:31:48.721 --> 00:31:51.424
Now remember, valence electro--
it's called the valence band.

00:31:51.424 --> 00:31:53.760
It makes sense because
valence electrons, remember,

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:56.896
are those ones, as we talked
about with Louis, where you've

00:31:56.896 --> 00:31:59.232
got kind of the bonding and
the action and the chemistry

00:31:59.232 --> 00:32:00.166
happening.

00:32:00.166 --> 00:32:03.236
And so this is
that top most state

00:32:03.236 --> 00:32:05.972
of the top most valence band.

00:32:05.972 --> 00:32:07.640
This is the valence
band top most state.

00:32:10.343 --> 00:32:12.912
And there's one more definition
we got to know about.

00:32:12.912 --> 00:32:14.347
This is why we're
setting this up,

00:32:14.347 --> 00:32:17.984
which is that that energy
difference here, oh, that

00:32:17.984 --> 00:32:20.320
is really important.

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:25.258
So the energy of the
conduction band minimum,

00:32:25.258 --> 00:32:29.228
minus the energy of the
valence band maximum,

00:32:29.228 --> 00:32:35.735
is equal to something called
the bandgap, energy gap.

00:32:35.735 --> 00:32:36.469
It's the band gap.

00:32:40.540 --> 00:32:46.346
This is an extremely important
property of materials,

00:32:46.346 --> 00:32:47.814
the electronic band gap.

00:32:50.750 --> 00:32:53.019
What is the energy difference
between the highest

00:32:53.019 --> 00:32:58.224
occupied electrons and the
lowest unoccupied electrons?

00:32:58.224 --> 00:33:01.327
That's what we're talking about.

00:33:01.327 --> 00:33:07.800
So if that filling,
if that distance--

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:10.637
OK, so here's an
element, carbon,

00:33:10.637 --> 00:33:12.505
in the diamond structure.

00:33:12.505 --> 00:33:17.443
And just-- purple is filled
and not purple is not filled.

00:33:17.443 --> 00:33:19.846
So you know that that's
the connection band.

00:33:19.846 --> 00:33:21.114
And that's the valence band.

00:33:21.114 --> 00:33:24.817
And the band gap, which
we've just defined here,

00:33:24.817 --> 00:33:29.122
is the energy difference
between those.

00:33:29.122 --> 00:33:31.124
And for diamond,
it's really large.

00:33:31.124 --> 00:33:33.459
For diamond, it's really large.

00:33:33.459 --> 00:33:37.797
So you can see that, in
fact, the gap is over--

00:33:37.797 --> 00:33:39.432
if we converted this
to electron-volts,

00:33:39.432 --> 00:33:42.468
is more than five
electron-volts.

00:33:45.505 --> 00:33:49.175
Now, I mentioned
thermal energy in answer

00:33:49.175 --> 00:33:52.345
to the question about water.

00:33:52.345 --> 00:33:58.651
And room temperature--
so room temperature,

00:33:58.651 --> 00:34:02.021
the energy of room temperature,
if you think about temperature

00:34:02.021 --> 00:34:07.927
as an energy of like vibrational
motion, and you can do that.

00:34:07.927 --> 00:34:11.496
You use something called
the Boltzmann constant

00:34:11.496 --> 00:34:14.033
and you can get what the
room temperature energy is.

00:34:14.033 --> 00:34:19.038
And it's something like
0.025 electron-volts.

00:34:19.038 --> 00:34:20.672
Seems pretty small.

00:34:20.672 --> 00:34:25.143
So the point is, if that's the
energy, the thermal energy I

00:34:25.143 --> 00:34:30.616
have at room temperature,
then if my bandgap is so much

00:34:30.616 --> 00:34:33.152
larger than that, is
so much larger than

00:34:33.152 --> 00:34:37.056
that, it means those electrons
are always going to be stuck.

00:34:37.056 --> 00:34:40.293
They're never going to,
even in some accident,

00:34:40.293 --> 00:34:44.630
have enough thermal energy
to come up and be excited.

00:34:44.630 --> 00:34:48.601
We'll talk about this later.

00:34:48.601 --> 00:34:49.902
So they're really stuck.

00:34:49.902 --> 00:34:53.072
And if an electron is
in the valence band,

00:34:53.072 --> 00:34:55.108
it can't move very well.

00:34:55.108 --> 00:34:58.377
It can't move-- it's kind
of stuck in those bonds.

00:34:58.377 --> 00:35:00.179
It's kind of stuck
in those bonds.

00:35:00.179 --> 00:35:04.317
And so here, if there is
electrons all stuck here,

00:35:04.317 --> 00:35:06.319
and they never have a
chance to go up here

00:35:06.319 --> 00:35:09.222
where they can
travel freely, this

00:35:09.222 --> 00:35:10.857
is really going to
be an insulator.

00:35:10.857 --> 00:35:15.194
This is not going to carry
electrons through the material.

00:35:15.194 --> 00:35:16.162
So that's an insulator.

00:35:16.162 --> 00:35:18.731
When you have a very
large bandgap like that,

00:35:18.731 --> 00:35:21.768
we call that an insulator.

00:35:21.768 --> 00:35:23.269
OK, but see if we compare--

00:35:23.269 --> 00:35:24.771
and this is
interesting, you're just

00:35:24.771 --> 00:35:28.508
going down in the periodic
table if you compare diamond

00:35:28.508 --> 00:35:32.645
to silicon to germanium.

00:35:32.645 --> 00:35:35.848
Well, see, what happens is
the bandgap gets smaller.

00:35:35.848 --> 00:35:39.886
I'm still filling them all the
way up to the top of a band.

00:35:39.886 --> 00:35:42.688
So I'm not a metal.

00:35:42.688 --> 00:35:45.258
If I'm a metal, it's because
my filling stopped somewhere

00:35:45.258 --> 00:35:46.793
in the middle of the band.

00:35:46.793 --> 00:35:48.294
I'm not a metal.

00:35:48.294 --> 00:35:51.430
I'm a semi conductor
and not an insulator.

00:35:51.430 --> 00:35:53.332
And the reason I'm
a semi conductor

00:35:53.332 --> 00:35:55.368
is that my bandgap
is kind of small

00:35:55.368 --> 00:35:59.172
enough so that electrons
can kind of jump from here

00:35:59.172 --> 00:36:03.409
up to there, sometimes.

00:36:03.409 --> 00:36:05.411
Sometimes.

00:36:05.411 --> 00:36:09.615
That's why it's called
a semi conductor.

00:36:09.615 --> 00:36:12.051
They kind of conduct electrons.

00:36:12.051 --> 00:36:15.988
So the electron can get
up to the conduction

00:36:15.988 --> 00:36:19.659
band in a semiconductor more
easily than an insulator.

00:36:19.659 --> 00:36:22.862
And that's why it can conduct
electricity much better

00:36:22.862 --> 00:36:27.366
than an insulator, many, many,
many orders of magnitude.

00:36:27.366 --> 00:36:29.368
But the way that we have
talked about getting

00:36:29.368 --> 00:36:33.239
electrons excited,
getting electrons excited,

00:36:33.239 --> 00:36:34.807
is with light.

00:36:34.807 --> 00:36:37.443
And we can do that
for solids, too.

00:36:37.443 --> 00:36:40.179
We can do the same
thing for solids.

00:36:40.179 --> 00:36:48.221
And so if I plot the
absorption, if I take a solid

00:36:48.221 --> 00:36:51.290
and I shine a light
on it, there's

00:36:51.290 --> 00:36:59.332
something called the absorption
coefficient, which is really

00:36:59.332 --> 00:37:06.005
just a measure of how
far down into the solid

00:37:06.005 --> 00:37:10.776
did that light have to go
before some electron got excited

00:37:10.776 --> 00:37:12.478
by it?

00:37:12.478 --> 00:37:14.881
How well does it-- well,
OK, different solids

00:37:14.881 --> 00:37:16.949
can absorb electrons
differently.

00:37:16.949 --> 00:37:21.220
But the main point is, if
I plot that versus energy

00:37:21.220 --> 00:37:27.827
for something like silicon, the
bandgap of silicon is 1.1 ev,

00:37:27.827 --> 00:37:29.695
so that's what it would
look like for silicon

00:37:29.695 --> 00:37:33.399
because you know--

00:37:33.399 --> 00:37:34.967
this should be wavelength.

00:37:38.905 --> 00:37:44.911
Because you know-- because
as you get to lower--

00:37:44.911 --> 00:37:48.447
so you know that above--

00:37:48.447 --> 00:37:50.449
it's exactly like for atoms.

00:37:50.449 --> 00:37:53.519
If I shine light, like
remember the Bohr model,

00:37:53.519 --> 00:37:57.890
if I shine light on an atom that
has enough energy to promote it

00:37:57.890 --> 00:38:01.560
up, then it can go.

00:38:01.560 --> 00:38:08.067
But the difference here is
that for silicon, for silicon,

00:38:08.067 --> 00:38:09.869
that's right there,
for silicon there's

00:38:09.869 --> 00:38:12.171
this big empty
space with no bands.

00:38:12.171 --> 00:38:13.673
It's just like the atom.

00:38:13.673 --> 00:38:18.811
When there's no states, I
cannot have an electron there.

00:38:18.811 --> 00:38:20.112
I'm stuck.

00:38:20.112 --> 00:38:22.682
So the electron can be
here or it can be here.

00:38:22.682 --> 00:38:24.417
And so the light, the
energy of the light,

00:38:24.417 --> 00:38:28.454
has to be at least the
bandgap to get it up.

00:38:28.454 --> 00:38:32.058
At least the bandgap
to get it up.

00:38:32.058 --> 00:38:34.994
So what that means
is that if I had--

00:38:34.994 --> 00:38:39.332
so, why does this go to zero
with increasing wavelength?

00:38:39.332 --> 00:38:43.869
What it means is that if my
valence band looks like this

00:38:43.869 --> 00:38:46.906
and my conduction
band looks like this,

00:38:46.906 --> 00:38:50.409
and now my light is coming
and I want to take an electron

00:38:50.409 --> 00:38:52.511
and promote it up.

00:38:52.511 --> 00:38:54.613
Well, it cannot be--

00:38:54.613 --> 00:39:04.790
the electron cannot exist
inside the band gap.

00:39:04.790 --> 00:39:06.459
There is no states there.

00:39:06.459 --> 00:39:08.227
It's just like we
talked about with atoms.

00:39:08.227 --> 00:39:11.197
But now we've got almost
infinite states here,

00:39:11.197 --> 00:39:12.365
no states there.

00:39:12.365 --> 00:39:14.200
And then again we start
with infinite places

00:39:14.200 --> 00:39:14.900
where it can be.

00:39:17.670 --> 00:39:19.872
What that means is that
if I shine light on it,

00:39:19.872 --> 00:39:24.577
the only way silicon, the only
way it can absorb this light

00:39:24.577 --> 00:39:29.081
is if the light has energy
greater than the band gap,

00:39:29.081 --> 00:39:31.917
or wavelength less
than the bandgap.

00:39:31.917 --> 00:39:34.086
That's why I drew it that way.

00:39:34.086 --> 00:39:35.521
So it goes to
zero-- so it's going

00:39:35.521 --> 00:39:37.790
to absorb in wavelength
up to a certain point.

00:39:37.790 --> 00:39:39.592
And then if I go
higher in wavelength,

00:39:39.592 --> 00:39:41.193
those are energies
that are too small.

00:39:41.193 --> 00:39:43.763
It's going to try to absorb here
but there's nowhere to absorb

00:39:43.763 --> 00:39:46.999
to, so it can't.

00:39:46.999 --> 00:39:49.368
But if it has enough
energy, oh, look at this.

00:39:49.368 --> 00:39:50.603
This is also different.

00:39:50.603 --> 00:39:54.306
I could absorb here
or here or here.

00:39:54.306 --> 00:39:57.009
I can take all those
higher energies

00:39:57.009 --> 00:40:01.247
and absorb light
kind of continuously,

00:40:01.247 --> 00:40:03.082
unlike in the atom
where it could only

00:40:03.082 --> 00:40:07.086
be the level to hop you from
n equals 2 to n equals 4.

00:40:07.086 --> 00:40:11.190
But here I have an almost
continuous set of states,

00:40:11.190 --> 00:40:15.428
except in the band gap,
where I have no states.

00:40:15.428 --> 00:40:19.098
So that's what the absorption
would look like for silicon.

00:40:19.098 --> 00:40:22.601
And if you look at the bandgap,
this very, very important

00:40:22.601 --> 00:40:24.403
quantity, there is carbon.

00:40:24.403 --> 00:40:25.838
There's diamond.

00:40:25.838 --> 00:40:27.039
There's silicon.

00:40:27.039 --> 00:40:29.075
There's germanium.

00:40:29.075 --> 00:40:32.978
And you can see that as you
go to heavier and heavier

00:40:32.978 --> 00:40:38.017
elements, you change the gap.

00:40:38.017 --> 00:40:39.585
And you can understand
this from what

00:40:39.585 --> 00:40:42.488
you know about the
electron levels in atoms.

00:40:42.488 --> 00:40:50.930
Because in an atom, remember,
if I get heavier and heavier,

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:55.334
those electrons all the way on
the outer edges of the atom,

00:40:55.334 --> 00:40:58.537
they're more weakly
bonded to the atom.

00:40:58.537 --> 00:41:02.575
They're weaker and
weaker and weaker.

00:41:02.575 --> 00:41:05.411
So the energy is
higher and higher.

00:41:05.411 --> 00:41:09.014
Remember, so instead of being
these deeper energy levels that

00:41:09.014 --> 00:41:12.318
have more bonding, more energy
in them, lower in energy,

00:41:12.318 --> 00:41:15.688
they're less and less energy.

00:41:15.688 --> 00:41:19.158
So you can imagine,
now, that those

00:41:19.158 --> 00:41:20.826
are what you're starting with.

00:41:20.826 --> 00:41:22.928
Those are what you're
starting with, these.

00:41:22.928 --> 00:41:25.397
Right these are the atom levels
that you're starting with.

00:41:25.397 --> 00:41:28.167
So if you're going to make
your atom have electrons that

00:41:28.167 --> 00:41:32.938
are more weakly bound with
energies that are higher,

00:41:32.938 --> 00:41:35.174
that aren't as deep and
don't have as much distance

00:41:35.174 --> 00:41:39.078
between them in the
energy landscape,

00:41:39.078 --> 00:41:41.013
then the gaps will get smaller.

00:41:41.013 --> 00:41:45.618
Now, that is a
very simple picture

00:41:45.618 --> 00:41:46.886
of why the gaps get smaller.

00:41:46.886 --> 00:41:49.522
It happens to work
for this column.

00:41:49.522 --> 00:41:51.790
So if you look at
this-- and some people

00:41:51.790 --> 00:41:54.527
would even call this
a metal because that's

00:41:54.527 --> 00:41:58.564
such a small gap, but I like
to call it a semiconductor.

00:41:58.564 --> 00:42:02.168
But if your gap is zero,
if you're band gap is zero,

00:42:02.168 --> 00:42:04.537
it means there is no gap.

00:42:04.537 --> 00:42:08.474
So by definition, you're
in the middle of a band.

00:42:08.474 --> 00:42:10.209
You're somewhere in
the middle of a band.

00:42:10.209 --> 00:42:13.579
And if you're in the middle
of a band, you're a metal.

00:42:13.579 --> 00:42:16.582
You didn't fill that
band all the way up.

00:42:16.582 --> 00:42:18.217
But if you filled it
up and then there's

00:42:18.217 --> 00:42:20.653
a little gap between
you and the next level,

00:42:20.653 --> 00:42:21.854
then you're a semiconductor.

00:42:21.854 --> 00:42:25.191
Or if it's much greater,
like greater than three

00:42:25.191 --> 00:42:28.060
electron volts, 3 and
1/2 electron volts,

00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:31.730
then you're an insulator.

00:42:31.730 --> 00:42:34.500
Now, so you can
imagine now why--

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:37.369
so as the interactions
get stronger,

00:42:37.369 --> 00:42:41.040
you can imagine those
distances can get larger.

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:44.143
And so you could have
larger and larger gaps,

00:42:44.143 --> 00:42:45.678
which can explain this trend.

00:42:45.678 --> 00:42:48.480
But as I said before,
where did I put it,

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:53.886
it depends, not just on the atom
and the original energy levels,

00:42:53.886 --> 00:42:56.222
but on how those bond together.

00:42:56.222 --> 00:42:58.357
And we'll talk about
that on Wednesday.

00:42:58.357 --> 00:43:00.492
And it also depends on
the structure, which

00:43:00.492 --> 00:43:01.994
we'll talk about next week.

00:43:01.994 --> 00:43:03.696
So it's not just
as simple as that

00:43:03.696 --> 00:43:07.233
but you can understand
basic trends this way

00:43:07.233 --> 00:43:09.568
for some of the columns.

00:43:09.568 --> 00:43:14.373
OK, we'll get to that on Friday.

00:43:14.373 --> 00:43:17.443
Now, the goodie bag gives
you a way to probe this.

00:43:17.443 --> 00:43:18.877
So what's in the goodie bag?

00:43:18.877 --> 00:43:24.450
The goodie bag has a way
of measuring conductivity.

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:27.453
Now, I just told you that you
cannot get connectivity unless

00:43:27.453 --> 00:43:32.725
you have electrons that
are able to make this jump,

00:43:32.725 --> 00:43:34.360
to make this jump.

00:43:34.360 --> 00:43:37.997
They've got to be able to
go up from the valence band

00:43:37.997 --> 00:43:39.531
to the conduction band.

00:43:39.531 --> 00:43:41.967
If they don't do that,
they're not free.

00:43:41.967 --> 00:43:44.503
They're stuck in these bonds.

00:43:44.503 --> 00:43:48.641
You've got to get them free so
they can roam around and not

00:43:48.641 --> 00:43:51.844
be stuck in this
bond or that bond.

00:43:51.844 --> 00:43:53.078
This measures that.

00:43:53.078 --> 00:43:54.179
This measures that.

00:43:54.179 --> 00:43:57.950
This measures whether electrons
are in the conduction band.

00:43:57.950 --> 00:44:01.287
You've also got some
LEDs and some LEDs.

00:44:01.287 --> 00:44:04.156
And this is a
critical piece here.

00:44:04.156 --> 00:44:08.127
Why do you have
two kinds of LEDs?

00:44:08.127 --> 00:44:11.196
Because on the one
hand, what you're doing,

00:44:11.196 --> 00:44:13.999
I'm giving you a way
to excite electrons.

00:44:13.999 --> 00:44:19.972
So I'm giving you a way to
actually promote electrons.

00:44:19.972 --> 00:44:23.676
So you've got 10 to the 24
electrons in this valence band.

00:44:23.676 --> 00:44:27.079
This would be the vb and
this would be the cb.

00:44:27.079 --> 00:44:31.817
And what you're doing
is you're shining light

00:44:31.817 --> 00:44:36.822
with different frequencies,
which means different energies.

00:44:36.822 --> 00:44:38.590
That's what the right
hand side gives you.

00:44:38.590 --> 00:44:42.661
It gives you different
energies of the photons.

00:44:42.661 --> 00:44:45.564
So you have a way,
now, of shining energy

00:44:45.564 --> 00:44:46.965
on a semiconductor.

00:44:46.965 --> 00:44:49.401
And that's the second piece.

00:44:49.401 --> 00:44:52.604
The semiconductor
is what an LED is.

00:44:52.604 --> 00:44:55.374
It's a semiconductor.

00:44:55.374 --> 00:44:57.509
If LEDs were insulators,
they wouldn't work.

00:44:57.509 --> 00:45:00.012
And if they were metals,
they wouldn't work.

00:45:00.012 --> 00:45:04.883
Because the way an LED works
is actually just the opposite.

00:45:04.883 --> 00:45:09.054
Here, I'm shining light so
that an electron gets promoted

00:45:09.054 --> 00:45:12.124
and I can measure that
with my voltmeter.

00:45:12.124 --> 00:45:16.161
But I could also put
electrons in with a current.

00:45:16.161 --> 00:45:17.996
That gets them up
here and then they

00:45:17.996 --> 00:45:20.032
drop down and emit a photon.

00:45:20.032 --> 00:45:22.434
That's what an LED is.

00:45:22.434 --> 00:45:24.770
That's what an LED is,
it's just the reverse.

00:45:24.770 --> 00:45:27.272
And so what I've
given you on the left

00:45:27.272 --> 00:45:30.809
are different semiconductors.

00:45:30.809 --> 00:45:34.146
Because you see, just like
a different-- you know,

00:45:34.146 --> 00:45:37.182
if I'm running current through
like I am on the right,

00:45:37.182 --> 00:45:39.251
you see, those are
different colors.

00:45:39.251 --> 00:45:44.590
That means that I must
have different gaps.

00:45:44.590 --> 00:45:45.224
You know that.

00:45:45.224 --> 00:45:46.658
You know that
already from the work

00:45:46.658 --> 00:45:50.362
we did with Bohr and the
color or frequency of light

00:45:50.362 --> 00:45:52.564
that you can get out of
different transitions.

00:45:52.564 --> 00:45:54.166
But it's the same here.

00:45:54.166 --> 00:45:57.169
Did an electron-- you know,
did I put an electron up here

00:45:57.169 --> 00:46:02.441
and then it fell down
and gave off some lambda?

00:46:02.441 --> 00:46:08.847
That lambda has to equal
the difference between the--

00:46:08.847 --> 00:46:10.849
it just equals the band gap.

00:46:10.849 --> 00:46:15.020
It equals the band gap because
the electron was put up

00:46:15.020 --> 00:46:17.856
to here in the cbm and then
it drops down to the vbm

00:46:17.856 --> 00:46:18.824
and it emits a photon.

00:46:18.824 --> 00:46:20.759
That's what's happening
on the right hand side.

00:46:20.759 --> 00:46:23.228
But see, you've also got,
on the left hand side,

00:46:23.228 --> 00:46:29.268
you've got sensors because
it's just LEDs on the left.

00:46:29.268 --> 00:46:33.305
But if I hook those
up, if I hook those up

00:46:33.305 --> 00:46:37.009
to the volt meter,
well, I'm hooking up

00:46:37.009 --> 00:46:39.011
a semiconductor to a voltmeter.

00:46:39.011 --> 00:46:44.183
So I'm hooking this up and I'm
reading, is there any current.

00:46:44.183 --> 00:46:46.985
And the answer in
a dark room is no,

00:46:46.985 --> 00:46:48.854
unless you make it
really, really hot.

00:46:48.854 --> 00:46:51.557
Don't, because you have to
go to very high temperatures.

00:46:51.557 --> 00:46:54.827
Well, what if I shine
light on this now?

00:46:54.827 --> 00:46:58.030
Well, if I shine light
that has the right energy

00:46:58.030 --> 00:47:02.334
to put electrons up in
the cbm from the vbm,

00:47:02.334 --> 00:47:04.803
then I'll read something
on the voltmeter.

00:47:04.803 --> 00:47:07.806
So by giving you different
LEDs on the left,

00:47:07.806 --> 00:47:09.575
I'm giving you
different band gaps.

00:47:09.575 --> 00:47:13.545
You have in your hands different
semiconductor band gaps.

00:47:13.545 --> 00:47:16.615
And by reading whether there
are electrons in here or not,

00:47:16.615 --> 00:47:19.785
you can tell what frequency
of light I shined on them.

00:47:19.785 --> 00:47:23.522
So they're both emitters
of photons and detectors

00:47:23.522 --> 00:47:24.990
of photons.

00:47:24.990 --> 00:47:27.693
And it all comes
together because you

00:47:27.693 --> 00:47:31.196
have a semiconductor, which
happens because you took 10

00:47:31.196 --> 00:47:33.832
to the 24 states, you
put them together,

00:47:33.832 --> 00:47:36.502
you had gaps between them,
and you filled all the way up

00:47:36.502 --> 00:47:40.806
to one of those gaps, and that
gap was around 1 or 2 electron

00:47:40.806 --> 00:47:42.241
volts.

00:47:42.241 --> 00:47:46.478
We will pick this up on
Friday and go further--

00:47:46.478 --> 00:47:48.080
I mean on Wednesday,
on Wednesday.

